Noctalia Greeter on Void Linux
==============================

1. Point greetd at the session wrapper in /etc/greetd/config.toml:

	[default_session]
	command = "/usr/bin/noctalia-greeter-session"
	user = "_greeter"

   Void's greetd package runs its greeter as "_greeter", not "greeter".
   /var/lib/noctalia-greeter is created owned by _greeter on install.

2. Initialize greeter state (as root, once):

	GREETER_USER=_greeter noctalia-greeter-apply-appearance --setup-system

3. Enable greetd, and disable any other display manager and the agetty
   service on greetd's VT (see /usr/share/doc/greetd/README.voidlinux):

	ln -s /etc/sv/greetd /var/service/

Session runtime directory (elogind)
-----------------------------------
Wayland sessions need XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (/run/user/<uid>). Noctalia's
appearance sync also requires it.

* With elogind (most desktop setups): install elogind, enable the dbus and
  elogind services, and make sure /etc/pam.d/greetd includes a session
  line for pam_elogind.so. Void's default greetd PAM file includes
  system-local-login, which pulls it in when elogind is installed.

* Without elogind: something else must create XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, e.g.
  pam_rundir (package "pam_rundir") added to /etc/pam.d/greetd as
  "session optional pam_rundir.so", or seatd + turnstile. Appearance sync
  with Noctalia will not work without a /run/user/<uid> directory.

Do NOT run /usr/share/noctalia-greeter/setup_greeter_system.sh blindly:
it edits /etc/pam.d/greetd directly. Steps 1-2 above cover what Void needs.

Optional: appearance sync with Noctalia
---------------------------------------
Needs polkit (provides pkexec):

	xbps-install polkit

Each sync asks for an admin password by default. To allow a user to sync
without a password (an explicit administrator choice):

	noctalia-greeter passwordless-sync enable <user>
